      body: `Comets reach us from two very different reservoirs in the outer Solar System, and the distinction encodes their formation history and dynamical evolution. Short-period comets — those with orbital periods under 200 years — trace back to the Kuiper Belt, the flattened disk of icy bodies between 30 and 50 AU. These comets are confined close to the ecliptic plane because their source disk is itself flat; they are delivered inward when Neptune's gravitational resonances or close encounters redirect a Kuiper Belt Object (KBO) onto a Sun-approaching orbit. "Jupiter-family" comets (periods under 20 years) like 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko are the most common visitors and show low orbital inclinations consistent with this disk origin. Long-period comets — those with periods exceeding 200 years — arrive from essentially random directions on the sky, including highly inclined and retrograde orbits, betraying their origin in the spherically distributed Oort Cloud. The Oort Cloud itself formed when Neptune and Uranus scattered the original population of outer Solar System planetesimals to enormous distances during their outward migration; these icy exiles were then further affected by galactic tidal forces that rounded their orbits into the spherical shell we infer today.

The ESA Rosetta mission transformed our understanding of cometary composition by spending over two years studying 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko at close range — a level of detail never previously achieved. Its ROSINA mass spectrometer measured the D/H ratio of 67P's water at approximately 3.0 × 10⁻⁴, roughly three times Earth's ocean value and definitively too high for Jupiter-family comets to have been the dominant supplier of Earth's water. Instead, the best compositional match comes from carbonaceous chondrite asteroids, shifting the scientific consensus toward asteroid-delivered water for Earth's oceans. Rosetta also found glycine, phosphine, and a rich inventory of organic molecules — acetaldehyde, acetone, propanol, and others — in 67P's coma, demonstrating that comets preserve and deliver chemistry directly relevant to the origin of life's building blocks. 67P's bilobed "rubber duck" nucleus, moreover, revealed that the comet consists of two separately formed objects that collided gently very early in Solar System history, a finding that speaks to the gentle, low-velocity accretion processes operating in the outer protoplanetary disk.`,
