          explain: 'Option A correctly identifies the solar wind as the mechanism driving the ion tail\'s anti-sunward orientation and accurately describes how the dust tail differs in both physical mechanism and morphology. The solar wind is a continuous outward flow of plasma — primarily protons and electrons — from the solar corona, reaching speeds of 400–800 km/s in the typical "slow" and "fast" stream regimes. This wind carries with it the interplanetary magnetic field (IMF), structured into the Parker spiral pattern from the Sun\'s rotation. When this magnetised plasma encounters a comet\'s coma, it interacts with the ionised gas molecules produced by solar UV photodissociation and photoionisation of water vapour, CO₂, and CO. The IMF "picks up" these newly formed ions and sweeps them anti-sunward at close to solar wind speed. Because this electromagnetic coupling acts instantaneously compared to the comet\'s orbital motion, the ion tail always points precisely radially away from the Sun regardless of the comet\'s direction of travel — if a comet is moving directly toward the Sun, its ion tail still points directly away from the Sun, behind the nucleus in the direction opposite the comet\'s motion. Disconnection events (DCEs) — in which the ion tail appears to detach from the head and a new tail regrows — occur when the IMF polarity reverses (a "sector boundary" crossing), momentarily disconnecting the tail from the solar wind pickup mechanism. The dust tail behaves entirely differently: dust particles ejected from the nucleus are neutral (uncharged) and are therefore not affected by the IMF. They are pushed away from the Sun by radiation pressure — the net momentum transferred by solar photons — but this force is weak compared to gravity for large particles. Additionally, the ejected dust particles retain much of the comet\'s orbital momentum at the moment of ejection. As the comet moves along its orbit, dust ejected at different times occupies different orbital positions, creating the curved, spread-out dust tail that follows the orbital path like a train behind the comet. The dust tail therefore lags behind the comet\'s direction of motion and curves away from the orbital path, appearing yellowish-white from reflected sunlight. When a comet is receding from perihelion, both tails point generally forward (ahead of the comet\'s direction of travel) — the dust tail slightly less so, curving between the orbital direction and the anti-sunward direction. This geometry provides a powerful visual diagnostic of the comet\'s orbital position and the relative importance of solar wind versus radiation pressure forces. Option B is entirely wrong: comet nuclei do not generate significant intrinsic magnetic fields; the Rosetta mission found no detectable magnetic field at 67P\'s nucleus; the "magnetic nozzle" concept described does not exist; and the Yarkovsky effect acts on solid bodies in orbit over years to decades, not on freshly ejected dust particles. Option C is incorrect in claiming both tails always point precisely anti-sunward: the ion tail does, but the dust tail curves away from the anti-sunward direction due to the combination of radiation pressure, gravity, and the comet\'s orbital momentum — the curvature is real, not a perspective illusion, and is routinely measured photometrically and modelled by syndyne-synchrone analysis. Option D is incorrect: the ion tail\'s direction is determined by the solar wind direction, not by the comet\'s velocity vector; fast-moving comets do not have forward-pointing tails; the consistent anti-sunward orientation has been confirmed for comets with widely varying velocities, directions, and distances from the Sun.',
