            'Jupiter-family comets like 67P were NOT the primary source of Earth\'s oceans; the D/H mismatch of ~3× means that if Earth\'s water came primarily from Jupiter-family comets, its oceans would be much more deuterium-enriched than they are; carbonaceous chondrites (C-type asteroid meteorites, especially CI type) have D/H ratios close to Earth\'s oceans (~1.4 × 10⁻⁴), making them the best compositional match; the current leading hypothesis is that Earth\'s water was delivered primarily by carbonaceous chondrite-like asteroids during the late heavy bombardment and during Earth\'s formation from the inner Solar System disk; the picture is not fully settled — Comet 103P/Hartley 2 (another Jupiter-family comet) has D/H closer to Earth values, and some Oort Cloud comets also have D/H near Earth\'s range — suggesting sources may be mixed; the total water budget also depends on what fraction survived the magma ocean stage versus what was delivered post-solidification',
