          correct: 0,
          explain: 'Option A correctly describes the contact-binary formation model for 67P and accurately represents the Rosetta findings. The most definitive evidence from Rosetta came from the OSIRIS camera system, which imaged 67P at resolutions down to a few centimetres per pixel over two years of orbital operations. Analysis of the internal layering structure visible in cliff faces, terraces, and outcrops on both lobes showed that the strata on each lobe are concentric around that lobe\'s own geometric centre — the layers curve around the head lobe independently and the layers on the body lobe curve around the body lobe\'s own centre. If 67P had originally been a single body that was later split and re-fused, or if it had been sculpted from a single body by erosion, the layering would be expected to follow a single overall centre of figure. The independently centred layering is a strong geometric argument that the two lobes formed and accreted separately in the protoplanetary disk and then collided at very low relative velocity — a few metres per second — in the early Solar System when planetesimals were abundant and collision velocities within the outer disk were low. This is consistent with the "pebble accretion" and "hierarchical accretion" models for outer Solar System planetesimal formation, in which large bodies built up through the gradual aggregation of smaller pebbles and sub-kilometre bodies in a process that favours low-velocity sticking rather than high-velocity shattering. 67P\'s bulk density of ~533 kg/m³ — about half the density of water — implies approximately 60–70% porosity by volume, confirming that the nucleus is not a solid consolidated body but a loosely bound aggregate held together by its own weak self-gravity and surface cohesion. This "rubble pile" structure is entirely consistent with a contact-binary origin. The bilobed morphology of 67P is not an isolated case: Earth-based radar observations and spacecraft flybys have revealed highly irregular, bilobed, or contact-binary shapes for a significant fraction of small Solar System bodies including asteroids (Kleopatra, Arrokoth/2014 MU₆₉ — visited by New Horizons in 2019 — is a spectacular example of a contact binary that formed by gentle accretion of two distinct lobes). Option B is incorrect: a high-velocity impact sufficient to split a cometary nucleus would generate enormous amounts of kinetic energy, producing a catastrophic disruption of the target body rather than a clean split into two retained fragments; the impact energies at km/s velocities are orders of magnitude above the gravitational binding energy of a loosely consolidated comet nucleus; furthermore, the independently centred layering would be destroyed or mixed by such an impact; the neck region shows no evidence of shock features expected from a high-velocity impact. Option C is incorrect: while preferential erosion of the neck region by sublimation is a real process (the neck is the most active region of 67P and does experience enhanced heating), it cannot produce the bilobed structure from an initially spherical or non-bilobed body — sublimation preferentially removes material from the most active surface facets and evolves the shape gradually, but the independently centred layering in both lobes predates any current erosion pattern and cannot be explained by surface erosion alone. Option D is incorrect: 67P is a Jupiter-family comet with an outer Solar System origin; the Yarkovsky effect is relevant for km-scale main belt asteroids but is negligibly small for the larger dynamical mechanisms (Jupiter resonances, planetary close encounters) that deliver comets from the Kuiper Belt to inner Solar System orbits; the compositional analysis by Rosetta is consistent with outer Solar System formation chemistry, not main belt asteroid composition.',
